Task 1: Create a New Project in the Controller
A project in Automation Controller is a logical collection of Ansible playbooks and related files. Projects serve as the foundation for organizing your automation content.

Subtask 1.1: Access the Automation Controller Interface
Open your web browser and navigate to the Automation Controller URL provided in your lab environment

Log in using the credentials provided:

Username: admin
Password: password (or as specified in your lab environment)
Once logged in, you should see the Automation Controller dashboard

Subtask 1.2: Navigate to Projects Section
In the left navigation menu, click on Projects
You will see a list of existing projects (if any)
Click the Add button (+ icon) to create a new project
Subtask 1.3: Configure Basic Project Settings
Fill in the following basic information:

Name: Web Server Configuration Project
Description: Project for managing web server configurations and deployments
Organization: Select Default (or your assigned organization)
Leave other fields at their default values for now

Do not click Save yet - we will configure the source control settings first

Task 2: Link the Project to a Git Repository
Linking your project to a Git repository enables version control and collaborative development of your Ansible content.

Subtask 2.1: Configure Source Control Type
In the project configuration form, locate the Source Control Type dropdown
Select Git from the available options
Additional fields will appear for Git configuration
Subtask 2.2: Set Up Git Repository Connection
Configure the following Git settings:

Source Control URL: https://github.com/ansible/ansible-examples.git
Source Control Branch/Tag/Commit: master
Source Control Credential: Leave blank (public repository)
Under Source Control Update Options, check the following boxes:

Clean: Removes any local modifications to the repository
Delete on Update: Deletes the local repository and re-clones it
Update Revision on Launch: Updates the repository before each job run
Subtask 2.3: Save and Sync the Project
Click Save to create the project
The project will be created and automatically attempt to sync with the Git repository
You should see a sync status indicator showing the progress
Wait for the sync to complete successfully (green checkmark icon)
Subtask 2.4: Verify Project Content
Once synced, click on the project name to view its details
Navigate to the Jobs tab to see any sync jobs that have run
The project should now contain the playbooks from the Git repository
Task 3: Create a Job Template for Running Playbooks
A job template defines how a playbook should be executed, including which inventory to use, what credentials to apply, and various execution parameters.

Subtask 3.1: Navigate to Job Templates
In the left navigation menu, click on Templates
Click the Add dropdown and select Add job template
Subtask 3.2: Configure Basic Job Template Settings
Fill in the following information:
Name: Deploy Apache Web Server
Description: Template for deploying and configuring Apache web server
Job Type: Run (default)
Inventory: Select Demo Inventory (or create a new one if needed)
Project: Select Web Server Configuration Project (the project we just created)
Subtask 3.3: Select Playbook and Configure Execution
Playbook: From the dropdown, select lamp_simple/site.yml

This playbook installs and configures a LAMP stack
Credentials:

Click the search icon next to Credentials
Select Demo Credential or create a new machine credential if needed
Options: Check the following boxes:

Enable Privilege Escalation: Allows the playbook to use sudo
Enable Provisioning Callbacks: Allows nodes to request job execution
Subtask 3.4: Configure Advanced Settings
Verbosity: Select 1 (Verbose) to see detailed output during execution

Job Slicing: Leave at 1 (no slicing needed for this lab)

Timeout: Set to 0 (no timeout limit)

Show Changes: Check this box to display what changes the playbook would make

Subtask 3.5: Save and Test the Job Template
Click Save to create the job template

Once saved, click the Launch button (rocket icon) to test the template

The job will start executing and you can monitor its progress in real-time

Review the job output to ensure it completes successfully

Task 4: Verify and Manage Your Automation Setup
Subtask 4.1: Review Project Synchronization
Navigate back to Projects
Click on your Web Server Configuration Project
Click the Sync button to manually update from Git
Monitor the sync job to ensure it completes successfully
Subtask 4.2: Create Additional Job Templates
Create a second job template with these settings:
Name: Database Configuration
Description: Template for database server setup
Project: Web Server Configuration Project
Playbook: Select another playbook from the repository
Inventory: Use the same inventory as before
Subtask 4.3: Organize Templates with Labels
Edit your job templates to add labels for better organization:

Click on a job template to edit it
In the Labels field, add relevant tags like web-server, production, lamp-stack
Save the changes
Use labels to filter and search for templates in the Templates list

Subtask 4.4: Set Up Scheduled Jobs (Optional)
Edit your Deploy Apache Web Server template
Click on the Schedules tab
Click Add to create a new schedule
Configure a schedule to run the job automatically:
Name: Weekly Web Server Update
Start Date/Time: Set to a future date
Repeat Frequency: Weekly
Time Zone: Select your local time zone
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Project Sync Failures
If your project fails to sync with Git:

Check Repository URL: Ensure the Git URL is correct and accessible
Verify Credentials: For private repositories, ensure proper credentials are configured
Network Connectivity: Verify the Automation Controller can reach the Git server
Branch/Tag Issues: Ensure the specified branch or tag exists in the repository
Job Template Execution Errors
If job templates fail to execute:

Inventory Issues: Verify that your inventory contains valid, reachable hosts
Credential Problems: Ensure the selected credentials have proper permissions
Playbook Syntax: Check that the selected playbook has valid YAML syntax
Privilege Escalation: Verify that privilege escalation is enabled if the playbook requires sudo
Common Error Messages
"Project sync failed"

# Check project sync status
# Navigate to Projects > Your Project > Jobs tab
# Review the sync job output for specific error details
"No hosts matched"

# Verify inventory configuration
# Ensure host patterns in playbook match inventory groups
# Check that inventory sync completed successfully
Best Practices
Project Organization
Use Descriptive Names: Choose clear, descriptive names for projects and templates
Implement Version Control: Always use Git repositories for project source control
Regular Syncing: Enable automatic syncing to keep projects up-to-date
Documentation: Include comprehensive descriptions for all projects and templates
Security Considerations
Credential Management: Use Automation Controller's credential system instead of hardcoding secrets
Access Control: Implement proper role-based access control (RBAC)
Audit Logging: Regularly review job execution logs for security monitoring
Secure Repositories: Use private repositories for sensitive automation content
